Le Morne Brabant is a basalt monolith rising 556 metres above the lagoon at the south-western tip of Mauritius. Its summit, over 12 hectares, is ringed by cliffs, and its steep slopes are full of caves and overhangs.

In the 18th and early 19th centuries, that very inaccessibility made the mountain a refuge for maroons — people who had escaped slavery. According to oral tradition, when police came up the mountain on 1 February 1835 to announce the abolition of slavery, those on the summit thought they were about to be recaptured and leapt to their deaths. The mountain has become a symbol of the fight for freedom, and the Le Morne Cultural Landscape was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2008.

## Hiking

A trail climbs the mountain from its eastern side. The lower part is an easy walk with views over the lagoon; the upper part is steep, with some scrambling, and access to it has been regulated at times.

## Good to know

- Start at dawn: the climb is exposed to the sun.
- Consider a licensed local guide, who can also tell the mountain's story.
